# Line endings.
#
# This repository is edited on Windows and runs on Linux. Git's default on
# Windows (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout, which is fine
# for a Windows editor and fatal for everything else here:
#
#   * bash reads `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and reports "$'\r': command not found"
#   * docker compose reads TX_HTTP_PORT=18080\r and binds a port named "18080\r"
#   * a CRLF Dockerfile entrypoint fails inside the image with no useful error
#
# So the working tree is pinned to LF for every text file, whatever the
# developer's core.autocrlf is set to. Windows editors have handled LF for
# twenty years; Linux has never handled CR.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files that actually break. Listed explicitly so the
# intent survives someone relaxing the blanket rule above.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.ini     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.env     text eol=lf
.env.*    text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
*.mako    text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf

# A .bat or .ps1 genuinely wants CRLF — it is only ever run by Windows.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.tar.gz binary
